"""Extractive-sector practice and community relations."""


def build(course, module):
    return course(97, 'Extractive Sector Social Work and Community Relations',
        'Extractive Sector\nSocial Work and\nCommunity Relations',
        'Map affected people and institutional responsibilities across an extractive project, then design a safe and accountable engagement response.', [
        module('Trace pressures across the project lifecycle',
            'Connect project stages to community risks, dependencies and responsible actors.',
            """Social workers in extractive settings help residents and institutions make sense of changes to everyday life. The lifecycle begins before production, when exploration can alter expectations about jobs and land. Construction can change mobility, rents and service demand. Operations can affect work patterns, habitats and access to resources. Closure can leave a shrinking labour market alongside unfinished commitments. Map the stage in the case and name the next decision that could change local options. [S89]

A project map and a community access map answer different questions. Nearby residents may share a road, stream or market with people who live farther away. Seasonal users might visit only during harvest, while a household's livelihood can depend on buyers who live outside the project boundary. List each relationship alongside its evidence. Include people who face a burden even when they hold no formal role or property. A carefully bounded map helps the team avoid counting a group twice while still recognising people who occupy several roles. [S85]

Distinguish an observed change, a reported experience and a hypothesis. A fictional clinic's attendance record may show that journeys changed, while caregivers explain how changed traffic conditions affect them. The records support follow-up questions about access. Mezewo and Emeodu use secondary material on Emohua to describe livelihood and advocacy concerns. Their work suggests locally meaningful questions about daily activities and institutions. It cannot tell the team what happened in this classroom case. [R97]

Put a responsible organisation beside each question. Name who can offer support, who can assess an environmental claim, who can change transport arrangements and who can negotiate project commitments. Date each answer. Make uncertainty visible so colleagues can revise the map when the project changes stage.""",
            'Early works at fictional Afirin Ridge shift a trading route. Only village residents appear on the operator’s stakeholder map.',
            'Map four affected groups and two institutions. Identify a plausible stage-specific burden, evidence needed and a responsible next contact.',
            'Include travelling traders, caregivers, other road users and residents. Describe direct and indirect effects and keep reported changes separate from confirmed evidence.',
            """
Trace the project stage. | A temporary exploration camp precedes the mine decision. Which project detail belongs in the first community map? | Early access and service changes from exploration. | Job losses at a confirmed closure. | Only permanent operating facilities. | Rehabilitation details due after production. | Exploration can affect communities before a production decision.
Map linked users. | A rerouted road serves growers living in a different settlement. Whose access should the project map include? | Growers who depend on the route. | Residents listed at the worksite only. | Registered contractors only. | A single representative chosen by the operator. | Impacts follow actual dependencies beyond a boundary.
Classify the evidence. | Drivers describe longer waits and supply no measured travel times. How should that entry be recorded? | Reported delay pending corroboration. | Confirmed mean delay. | Proven access restoration. | An exact causal estimate. | Testimony can motivate verification while retaining its status.
Use contextual studies appropriately. | What can the Emohua thematic study contribute to an assessment of Afirin Ridge? | Contextual questions to investigate locally. | A measured estimate for Afirin Ridge. | Proof of the current clinic’s performance. | A duty list for the fictional operator. | A secondary local study informs enquiry without determining another site’s results.
Assign authority. | Which entry best closes a map line about disputed environmental harm? | Identify the qualified assessment authority and evidence needed. | Ask the facilitator to issue a technical finding. | Record all competing explanations as proven. | Assign the same contact to every decision. | Assigning the correct authority makes the follow-up usable.
"""),
        module('Plan participation around real barriers',
            'Design a participation route that reaches affected people and records how their input changes decisions.',
            """Start with the decision and what a person can influence. A project team may be able to change meeting arrangements and a proposed route while a permit decision belongs to another institution. Say which choices are open, who owns each decision and when input can change it. Share a short explanation before asking someone to respond. Giving realistic scope supports meaningful participation and avoids creating expectations that the facilitator cannot fulfil. [S89]

Check barriers from the standpoint of different participants. Some people can attend an evening meeting, while carers, shift workers or people without transport may need other times or accessible locations. Tenants may worry about consequences if they discuss land access in front of a landlord. Explain the available ways to contribute and establish a protected route for sensitive concerns. Seek input separately from workers who are hired through labour intermediaries if public disclosure of their role could make participation unsafe. Record only the information needed for a decision. [S85]

Measure influence by reading the decision record. A sign-in sheet can show contact, although it does not establish informed agreement. Compare the original options with the revised memo. Identify the point at which evidence from a group changed a route, schedule or commitment. When an option stays the same, give a reason that refers to the actual suggestion and name a review route if the person believes the account is inaccurate. Check that the response reaches people through the route they agreed to use. [S89]

Arhin’s comparison of communities facing displacement in a Ghanaian lithium project illustrates why negotiation conditions can vary even within one project. Ask which groups can question an assumption, obtain independent support and speak without fearing a material cost. Use these questions to test the design of your own fictional meeting. [R95]""",
            'Afirin Ridge holds its only meeting at shift change. The minutes report unanimous support because no objections were recorded.',
            'Draft two participation routes and a record for responding to a route proposal. Distinguish attendance from evidence of agreement.',
            'Specify influence and timing, offer a safe option for omitted people, and document a response to each substantive point with a review opportunity.',
            """
Explain open decisions. | When a team can change the meeting date but cannot issue a permit, what belongs in the invitation? | State each decision owner and the influence participants can have. | Describe the consultation as the permit decision. | Promise every requested outcome. | Send only the address and organiser name. | Clear scope allows people to decide whether and how to take part.
Check time barriers. | Two shifts miss every scheduled meeting. What is the best response? | Offer another contact route at suitable times. | Count them as accepting the decision. | Add names of peers who attended. | Increase the length of the same meeting. | An alternative requires testing against the observed timing barrier.
Protect sensitive input. | Tenants are reluctant to raise access concerns in front of lessors. Which route is preferable? | A private channel with explained confidentiality limits. | A shared form listing tenant names. | An open show of hands only. | A public email copied to landowners. | The channel must reduce avoidable disclosure and explain its limits.
Record influence. | Which evidence shows participation affected a haul-route decision? | The revised decision memo traces a changed option to residents’ evidence. | The hall reached capacity. | The photographer attended. | The sign-in form added a column. | A decision trail shows how input was considered.
Compare bargaining conditions. | Two fictional villages receive an identical presentation and respond differently. What should be compared? | Capacity to question terms, get advice and speak safely. | Number of slides in the common deck. | Travel distance for the facilitator only. | A single score assigned before engagement. | Identical information does not equal identical influence or resources.
"""),
        module('Coordinate support and institutional follow-up',
            'Write a bounded social work response with consent, escalation and a documented feedback loop.',
            """A community relations issue can contain several requests. A household may need support reaching a service, neighbours may want an operator to explain an access change, and a public institution may need to assess a disputed claim. Break the case into tasks that can move in parallel. Make a short statement about what the social work team can control, and list decisions requiring another authority. Include the affected person in choosing a contact method and agreeing how progress will be confirmed. [R97]

Use case information in proportion to the task. An aggregate description can show that a service is hard to reach without identifying individual patients. If a person agrees to a referral, tell them what will be shared, with whom and for what purpose. Explain relevant confidentiality limits and use the appropriate agency process. Keep identifying details separate from a community issue log. Ask the receiver to confirm receipt or advise if the task belongs elsewhere. Avoid making promises about clinical, technical or statutory outcomes. [S85]

Handle concern about the operator through an accessible process with a route to question how it responds. The UN Guiding Principles describe business due diligence, remediation and conditions for effective non-judicial mechanisms. As a practitioner, document the requested response and the relevant action owner. Keep an external route available. If a team has a financial relationship with the operator, disclose it at the point where it could affect confidence in the response. Arrange independent handling when impartiality is at risk. [S86]

Set a feedback date even when an answer is uncertain. Ask whether the person received the service or could act on the information. Record partial completion, reasons and what should happen next. Where repeated reports point to a shared barrier, raise the aggregate issue with the responsible body and share an update through the agreed channel.""",
            'A household misses appointments after a shuttle changes route. A project officer wants the names of every affected patient.',
            'Separate support and accountability tasks. Write an appropriate data-sharing statement and a follow-up plan.',
            'Use aggregate evidence for the transport concern, share only necessary authorised referral details, and confirm service receipt with a review date.',
            """
Separate requests. | Which plan serves a household referral and a wider shuttle complaint? | Track confidential support and an aggregate service issue in parallel. | Send a public table of patient details. | Wait for the wider issue before discussing support. | Use the same consent entry for every affected household. | Individual support and system change use different records and permissions.
Limit disclosure. | What should a route planner receive to assess scheduling barriers? | Aggregated patterns relevant to the timing decision. | Named service records for all users. | Referral narratives copied from the clinic. | Entire household histories with names removed only. | Share information proportionate to the operational question.
Check completion. | A receiving agency acknowledges a support referral. How should the case record be updated? | Acknowledged, with an agreed check of actual access. | Service completed on the acknowledgement date. | Closed unless the agency rejects it. | Counted as restored access for the whole community. | Acknowledgement and receiving help are separate milestones.
Disclose conflicts. | A practitioner’s agency is paid by the operator in a disputed case. What supports impartial review? | Disclose the relationship and offer independent handling. | Refer every issue to the same account manager. | Share contractual commercial details with residents. | Record the funder only after case closure. | A disclosed conflict and independent route support confidence.
Set feedback. | A decision is delayed and the person has already waited two weeks. What should the worker communicate? | The current position, who owns the next step and when to expect an update. | A final decision date selected without authority. | A request to restart the case from the beginning. | A statement that the case is done because it was logged. | A dated next step supports follow-up without false promises.
"""),
    ])
